# Deep beginner lessons, part 4: Q45-Q58.
$LEARN[45] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> CLI working modes form a ladder of commitment. Normal mode acts as it thinks. <b>Plan mode</b> thinks first, writes the thinking to <b>plan.md</b>, and stops: the file is the deliverable, reviewable in your editor, diffable, committable. Only your explicit next instruction moves it to branch, PR or implementation. Every other option describes climbing the ladder unasked: creating /copilot/plan branches, opening PRs with the plan as a comment, or beginning implementation all act beyond planning. <b>Example:</b> you run plan mode on 'migrate auth to the new provider'. It writes plan.md with phases, risks and test strategy, then idles. You review over coffee, adjust two phases, and only then say implement. Had it branched or PR-ed on its own, your review would chase moving code instead of judging a document. Planning-only is a promise, and plan.md is its receipt.</div>"
$LEARN[46] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Parallel agents sharing one file need three guarantees simultaneously, and each distractor drops exactly one. <b>Isolated branches</b> give each agent its own workspace so edits never interleave mid-flight. <b>Concurrency</b> (both keep running) preserves speed: serializing via a concurrency group would be safe but slow, violating the requirements. <b>Pre-merge overlap detection</b> (a required status check comparing the two PRs file lists) catches the shared/config.yaml collision before either merges. <b>Example:</b> agent1 and agent2 branch separately and both touch shared/config.yaml. The check fails on both PRs with 'overlap detected': a human reconciles the file once, both rebase, both merge green. CODEOWNERS review alone would approve sequentially without ever isolating the work; read-only tools would prevent the work itself; a concurrency group would make one agent wait idle. Check which guarantee each option sacrifices.</div>"
$LEARN[47] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> VS Code custom-agent handoffs have four fields with four jobs. <b>label</b> is button text. <b>agent</b> is the target identifier, and identifiers are <b>file-name stems</b>: planner.agent.md is 'planner', implementer.agent.md is 'implementer'. This is the exam favorite: frontmatter <b>name</b> values (IMPLEMENTER, code-review) are display metadata, not identifiers. <b>prompt</b> prefills the next chat. <b>send</b> decides auto-submit (true) versus staged button (false, the default). <b>model</b> takes the qualified Model (vendor) form like GPT-5.2 (copilot). <b>Example:</b> agent: implementer resolves because the file exists; agent: code-review dangles because no code-review.agent.md exists (review.agent.md stem is 'review'). Selecting Start Implementation with send: true therefore switches to implementer and fires the prompt under GPT-5.2 immediately. When a handoff misbehaves, compare the agent value against file stems first.</div>"
$LEARN[48] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> A <b>concurrency group</b> is a named queue: runs sharing a key execute one at a time (older ones cancel or queue). The key expression designs the queue topology. <b>github.ref</b> identifies the branch or PR ref, so grouping by workflow-plus-ref makes one queue per branch: pushes to PR-12 serialize while PR-12 and PR-13 run parallel. The group must be declared at <b>workflow level</b> to govern whole runs; job-level concurrency scopes single jobs and leaves cross-push overlap intact. Any key containing <b>run_id</b> is unique per run, producing one queue per run: mathematically no grouping at all. matrix parallelism limits fan-out, it never groups. <b>Example:</b> Copilot pushes three rapid commits to one PR branch: with group workflow-plus-ref, run 1 finishes, then 2, then 3, each testing the latest tree. With a run_id key all three overlap, waste runners, and race each other. Read the key as the queue definition.</div>"
$LEARN[49] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Multi-agent orchestration has two registries beginners swap. The <b>agents array</b> in an Orchestrator frontmatter lists subagents the model may invoke programmatically during a run: this is the machine call graph. <b>Handoffs</b> list buttons a human clicks in chat to move between agents: this is the human navigation menu. The <b>tools</b> list holds tool names, and subagents are agents, not tools. The Reviewer agent profile is irrelevant because the Reviewer invokes nothing. <b>Example:</b> Orchestrator frontmatter agents: [Explorer, Modifier, Summarizer] lets the model call all three in sequence with zero clicks. Adding Summarizer to Reviewer handoffs would only paint a button nobody presses mid-run; adding it to tools would be a type error (Summarizer is not a tool). When invocation fails, check the machine registry (agents), not the human menu (handoffs).</div>"
$LEARN[50] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The audit log is a database you query with field filters, and <b>action:</b> filters by event type. GitHub names deletion of an artifact the <b>artifact.destroy</b> event (family artifact, verb destroy), so action:artifact.destroy returns exactly those rows with actor and timestamp. The distractors query different dimensions: <b>repo:org/repo</b> slices by repository instead of by event (right database, wrong column), <b>action:workflows.run</b> matches run events rather than deletions, <b>operation:remove</b> is not valid filter syntax at all. <b>Example:</b> pasting action:artifact.destroy into the audit search instantly lists every deleted artifact org-wide this month; adding repo: narrows to one repo. Learn the two axes (action for what happened, repo/actor for where/who) and every audit question reduces to picking the right axis.</div>"
$LEARN[51] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Subagent frontmatter carries three independent gates; the failure names exactly one. <b>agents[]</b> on the Orchestrator: callable subagents (Explorer, Modifier run, so this registry works). <b>user-invocable: false</b> on Summarizer: blocks humans from @-mentioning it, irrelevant to model calls. <b>disable-model-invocation: true</b> on Summarizer: blocks the model from calling it, matching the symptom precisely (siblings run, Summarizer never starts). Missing handoffs cannot matter because handoffs serve chat buttons and this run is programmatic; missing editing tools cannot matter because summarizing needs only fetch. <b>Example debugging order:</b> siblings work therefore the caller and registry are fine; the failing child differs by exactly one flag; that flag names the gate. Diff the siblings, read the differing flag, done: disable-model-invocation.</div>"
$LEARN[52] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Multi-job agent pipelines are DAGs plus queues. <b>needs: [spec_analyzer, risk_reviewer]</b> on plan_merger draws two dependency arrows: the merger starts only after both analyzers finish, which is the fan-out (parallel analysis) then fan-in (single merge) shape. Without needs, all three would race and the merger would read half-written inputs. <b>Workflow-level concurrency</b> with a per-ref group (<b>multiagent-github.ref</b>) adds the queue dimension: different branches flow in parallel while rapid pushes to one branch serialize instead of overlapping. Job-level concurrency would scope the wrong level and leave cross-push races. <b>Example:</b> two PRs arrive together: four analyzer jobs run (two per PR), each PR merger waits for its own pair, and a second push to PR-7 queues behind its first run instead of corrupting it. needs orders within a run; concurrency orders runs against each other.</div>"
$LEARN[53] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Fencing a planner agent has two halves and the exhibit tests both. The <b>tools</b> half must contain only read-only capabilities: <b>search</b> (find), <b>read</b> (inspect), <b>fetch</b> (retrieve URLs). Every alternative smuggles write power: edit and commit write files, push/merge/publish mutate remotes or releases. One write tool anywhere reopens the exact misbehavior (editing before approval). The <b>handoff</b> half is governed by <b>send</b>: false (the default) stages the handoff as a button the human fires after approving the plan; true auto-submits the moment the plan text lands, before any human sees it. <b>Example:</b> with [search,read,fetch] plus send:false, the planner drafts, you read the plan, click Start Implementation, and only then does implementer touch code. Swap in publish or flip send to true and either the planner or the handoff acts pre-approval. Fence the tools, gate the transition.</div>"
$LEARN[54] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Human-gated agent execution is a three-act pipeline and order is load-bearing. <b>Act 1, the plan job</b> runs first and uploads the plan as a workflow artifact: the thing to be approved must exist as a reviewable object. <b>Act 2, the plan-review environment</b> with <b>SG_Review as required reviewers</b> is the lock: GitHub environments pause jobs until named humans approve, and only SG_Review satisfies 'only SG_Review approves plan outputs'. <b>Act 3, the execute job</b> declares <b>needs: [plan]</b> (runs after the plan) and <b>environment: plan-review</b> (waits behind the lock). The distractors each break one act: auto-advance removes the lock, a main-branch 2-approval ruleset guards pushes instead of plan outputs, SG_Dev reviewers violate the only-SG_Review rule. <b>Example:</b> agent drafts a migration plan, SG_Review gets a pending-deployment notice, a senior approves, and only then does execute run the migration. Produce, gate, execute: in that order, always.</div>"
$LEARN[55] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Agents go where their credentials allow, not where prose tells them. agent1 read billing-service because the credential it runs with reaches beyond product-api. Fixes divide into enforced versus wished. A <b>fine-grained PAT scoped to product-api</b> is enforced: API calls to other repos fail at authentication, so the scoping issue disappears at the credential layer regardless of agent behavior. A prose instruction ('never touch billing-service') is wished: models can and do ignore guidance under task pressure. A <b>workflow permissions block</b> scopes GITHUB_TOKEN, a different credential than the agent session uses. A <b>bot-push ruleset</b> on the other repos blocks one identity pushes only: reads continue, and the agent may not push as that bot anyway. <b>Example:</b> with the scoped PAT, agent1 asking for a billing-service file gets a 404 from the API: the failure is structural, auditable, and permanent. Scope the credential, not the conversation.</div>"
$LEARN[56] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Delegation safety tracks accountability, not difficulty. Agents excel at <b>generating</b> (assessment.md drafts), <b>analyzing</b> (dependency review of plan.md) and <b>checking</b> (does tasks.md exist): all reversible, all verifiable. Two actions are unsafe because no agent self-check substitutes for them. <b>Validating assessment accuracy</b> when 47 findings include a security vulnerability means judging truth with breach consequences: a wrong 'all clear' ships a vulnerability. <b>Approving Git commits</b> is the merge gate: approval publishes code to the shared tree. Both need an accountable human. <b>Example:</b> the agent drafts the assessment (safe), flags a possible auth bypass (analysis, safe), you verify the bypass is real (human judgment, unsafe to delegate), then you approve the fix commits (human gate). Generate freely, judge and release personally.</div>"
$LEARN[57] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The Copilot CLI agent harness validates generated shell commands through a guardrail file in its own format: <b>.copilot/hooks.yaml</b> with version, hooks (name, event, language, run script) and a cli section pointing at it. The script receives the candidate command in <b>$COPILOT_COMMAND</b> and vetoes with nonzero exit: here a grep rejects absolute paths and parent traversal (..), blocking every path escape outside the repo. Two choices are load-bearing. <b>event: pre-exec</b> runs validation before execution: post-exec would judge damage already done. The <b>config path</b> must be the harness file: a same-shaped YAML under .github/workflows/ is a CI workflow the CLI never reads. <b>Example:</b> the agent emits 'cat ../../etc/passwd': pre-exec catches the traversal, prints the guardrail message, exits 1, command never runs. Validate before, in the file the harness reads.</div>"
$LEARN[58] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> <b>Autonomy levels</b> trade speed against control on a fixed ladder. Read-only analysis cannot change anything (safe, useless for deploys). Non-production autonomy ships to staging freely (fast feedback, zero prod risk). <b>Human-in-the-loop with required manual approvals</b> lets the agent prepare everything but blocks production effects until named humans approve (the compliance posture: automation proposes, humans dispose). Fully autonomous execution removes every gate (fastest, unacceptable where secrets and production meet compliance). The requirement (agent updates and deploys workflows touching production secrets, compliance-sensitive) pins exactly one rung. <b>Example:</b> the agent drafts the workflow change and stages it to the production environment, which pauses showing 'waiting for review': a release manager approves after checking the diff, and only then do secrets flow. Read the requirement as constraints (production? secrets? compliance?), then pick the lowest rung satisfying all three.</div>"
